  "name": "Question 8",
  "problem": "Evaluate the double integral ${\u222c}_{R}\\frac{1+x\\operatorname{sin}\u2061y}{x}\\,\\text{d}A$,  where $R=[1,e]\\times [0,\\pi ].$",
  "worked_solution": "Using the linearity property of double integrals, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\u222c}_{R}\\frac{1+x\\operatorname{sin}\u2061y}{x}\\,\\text{d}A & ={\u222c}_{R}(\\frac{1}{x}+\\operatorname{sin}\u2061y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\u222c}_{R}\\frac{1}{x}\\text{\u00a0}\\text{d}A+{\u222c}_{R}\\operatorname{sin}\u2061y\\text{\u00a0}\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function under the first integral is independent of $y.$ So, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\u222c}_{R}\\frac{1}{x}\\text{\u00a0}\\text{d}A & =(\\pi -0){\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi \\cdot [\\operatorname{ln}\u2061|x|]_{1}^{e} \\\\  & =\\pi (\\operatorname{ln}\u2061e-\\operatorname{ln}\u20611) \\\\  & =\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function under the second integral is independent of $x.$ So, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\u222c}_{R}\\operatorname{sin}\u2061y\\text{\u00a0}\\text{d}A & =(e-1){\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}\u2061y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =(e-1)[-\\operatorname{cos}\u2061y]_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =(e-1)(-\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\pi +\\operatorname{cos}\u20610) \\\\  & =2(e-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, combining our results, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\u222c}_{R}\\frac{1+x\\operatorname{sin}\u2061y}{x}\\,\\text{d}A=\\pi +2(e-1)=2e+\\pi -2.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
